1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,249
By the end, one press switches the red light, and the next press switches it back.

2
00:00:05,249 --> 00:00:08,260
Your terminal also tells you the button was pressed.

3
00:00:08,760 --> 00:00:12,680
We'll read every line that makes it happen, and I'll tell you where each thing is.

4
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:20,708
Open your IEEE projects folder. Inside, open FRDM KL26Z Projects. Next, open P1

5
00:00:20,708 --> 00:00:24,800
Sensor Dashboard. Inside that, open demo code.

6
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:33,418
Then open 01 button polling vs interrupt. We need two files here. The first is

7
00:00:33,418 --> 00:00:37,060
gpio_input_interrupt.c: the lesson.

8
00:00:37,560 --> 00:00:41,960
The second is pin_mux.c: the list of pin jobs.

9
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:49,345
In MCUXpresso, look at the top left. That's the Project Explorer. Find your P1

10
00:00:49,345 --> 00:00:50,000
project.

11
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:54,741
If you haven't made it yet, watch the setup video, then come back. Click the little

12
00:00:54,741 --> 00:00:56,580
arrow beside the project to open it.

13
00:00:57,080 --> 00:01:01,355
Open the source folder. Double-click your main file. It opens in the middle of the

14
00:01:01,355 --> 00:01:01,720
screen.

15
00:01:02,720 --> 00:01:09,804
Open the lesson file with File, then Open File, in the top menu. Click inside it.

16
00:01:09,804 --> 00:01:13,040
Press Command A to select everything.

17
00:01:13,540 --> 00:01:19,854
Press Command C to copy. On Windows, use Control. Click the tab with your main file.

18
00:01:19,854 --> 00:01:22,260
Press Command A, then Command V.

19
00:01:22,760 --> 00:01:24,600
Then Command S to save.

20
00:01:25,100 --> 00:01:30,558
Now the second file. Open the lesson's pin_mux.c, select all, and copy. In your

21
00:01:30,558 --> 00:01:32,700
project, open the board folder.

22
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:39,120
Double-click pin_mux.c. Select all, paste, and save. Leave pin_mux.h alone.

23
00:01:40,120 --> 00:01:44,600
Let's peek at that pin file. Scroll to the function called BOARD InitPins.

24
00:01:45,100 --> 00:01:50,910
The three Clock Enable Clock lines switch on ports A, C and E. A port is a group of

25
00:01:50,910 --> 00:01:51,260
pins.

26
00:01:51,760 --> 00:01:53,760
A port with no clock is asleep.

27
00:01:54,260 --> 00:01:58,020
The two port A lines give two pins the job of sending and receiving text.

28
00:01:58,520 --> 00:02:04,200
Next is a small settings list for the button pin. Pull up gently holds the pin high.

29
00:02:04,700 --> 00:02:08,780
Mux as GPIO makes it a plain input or output pin.

30
00:02:09,280 --> 00:02:19,440
Port Set Pin Config applies that list to port C, pin 3. That is our button.

31
00:02:19,940 --> 00:02:29,220
Port Set Pin Mux for port E, pin 29, makes the red light's pin a plain pin.

32
00:02:29,720 --> 00:02:35,320
The last lines connect the text messages to those two port A pins. Close the file.

33
00:02:36,320 --> 00:02:40,480
Back in your main file. Look at the line numbers down the left side of the editor.

34
00:02:40,980 --> 00:02:45,903
Green boxes that begin with a slash and a star are notes for you. The chip never

35
00:02:45,903 --> 00:02:46,580
reads them.

36
00:02:47,080 --> 00:02:52,622
Lines beginning with two slashes are switched off. To switch lines on, drag to

37
00:02:52,622 --> 00:02:57,240
select them, then press Command slash. On Windows, Control slash.

38
00:02:58,240 --> 00:03:03,797
These seven lines are already on. Include means: bring in a list of names so we can

39
00:03:03,797 --> 00:03:04,400
use them.

40
00:03:04,900 --> 00:03:09,380
Line 89, debug console: gives us print f, for sending text.

41
00:03:09,880 --> 00:03:14,200
Line 90, port: lets us choose when a pin rings the doorbell.

42
00:03:14,700 --> 00:03:19,180
Line 91, gpio: lets us read and write pins.

43
00:03:19,680 --> 00:03:25,520
Line 92, common: shared tools, such as switching interrupts on.

44
00:03:26,020 --> 00:03:30,500
Line 93, board: knows where this board's button and lights are.

45
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:37,080
Lines 94 and 95: the pin setup and the clock setup.

46
00:03:38,080 --> 00:03:45,597
Find Step 1, around line 140. Select lines 140 through 157 and switch them on. Then

47
00:03:45,597 --> 00:03:47,680
we'll read them slowly.

48
00:03:48,180 --> 00:03:55,988
Line 140. Define creates a nickname. Whenever we write board LED GPIO, the computer

49
00:03:55,988 --> 00:03:58,340
reads board LED red GPIO.

50
00:03:58,840 --> 00:04:04,600
Line 141. The same for the pin number. The red light is pin 29.

51
00:04:05,100 --> 00:04:11,660
Line 143. The button's GPIO block. For our button, that is port C.

52
00:04:12,160 --> 00:04:15,920
Line 144. The button's port, used for interrupt settings.

53
00:04:16,420 --> 00:04:19,540
Line 145. The button's pin number: three.

54
00:04:20,040 --> 00:04:25,960
Line 146. The button's interrupt number, so the chip knows which doorbell.

55
00:04:26,460 --> 00:04:31,980
Line 147. The name of the function that answers that doorbell.

56
00:04:32,480 --> 00:04:36,800
Line 148. The button's name as text, so we can print it.

57
00:04:37,800 --> 00:04:44,040
Read it from right to left. It starts false. Its name is g Button Press; the g

58
00:04:44,040 --> 00:04:47,240
reminds us the whole program can see it.

59
00:04:47,740 --> 00:04:50,860
bool means it holds only true or false.

60
00:04:51,360 --> 00:04:56,635
volatile warns the compiler: this can change at a surprising moment, so check it

61
00:04:56,635 --> 00:04:57,360
every time.

62
00:04:57,860 --> 00:05:00,580
What value does it hold before anyone presses?

63
00:05:01,080 --> 00:05:06,080
Your turn. Pause the video.

64
00:05:06,080 --> 00:05:07,200
False.

65
00:05:08,200 --> 00:05:13,595
Line 152. This function is the interrupt handler. void means it hands nothing back.

66
00:05:13,595 --> 00:05:17,560
The second void means it takes nothing in. You never call it.

67
00:05:18,060 --> 00:05:19,020
The hardware does.

68
00:05:19,520 --> 00:05:27,680
Lines 153 and 157 are curly brackets. They mark where the function begins and ends.

69
00:05:28,180 --> 00:05:33,620
Line 154 tells the hardware: I heard the bell, clear it.

70
00:05:34,120 --> 00:05:40,402
Inside line 154, one U shifted left by the pin number builds a number with a single

71
00:05:40,402 --> 00:05:43,960
one in position three. It selects only our pin.

72
00:05:44,460 --> 00:05:49,420
Line 156 raises the flag: g Button Press becomes true.

73
00:05:49,920 --> 00:05:55,040
Notice what is missing: no printing and no waiting. A handler should be quick.

74
00:05:56,040 --> 00:06:05,795
Line 163, int main, is where your program starts. Line 164 opens it. Its closing

75
00:06:05,795 --> 00:06:08,600
bracket is at line 331.

76
00:06:09,600 --> 00:06:13,760
Select lines 186 to 188 and switch them on.

77
00:06:14,260 --> 00:06:19,700
Line 186, BOARD InitPins, runs the pin list we just read.

78
00:06:20,200 --> 00:06:25,720
Line 187, BOARD Boot Clock RUN, sets the heartbeat of the chip.

79
00:06:26,220 --> 00:06:32,318
Line 188, BOARD Init Debug Console, opens the text line. It needs the heartbeat

80
00:06:32,318 --> 00:06:35,020
first, which is why it comes third.

81
00:06:35,520 --> 00:06:41,440
The empty brackets mean: nothing to hand in. The semicolon ends the instruction.

82
00:06:42,440 --> 00:06:45,960
Switch on lines 213 and 214.

83
00:06:46,460 --> 00:06:53,100
Line 213 prints a title. The backslash r and backslash n move to a new line.

84
00:06:53,600 --> 00:06:59,660
Line 214 has a percent s: an empty slot for text. After the comma comes the text

85
00:06:59,660 --> 00:07:02,160
that fills it, the button's name.

86
00:07:02,660 --> 00:07:05,220
What will line 214 print?

87
00:07:05,720 --> 00:07:10,720
Your turn. Pause the video.

88
00:07:10,720 --> 00:07:13,920
Press SW1 to turn on or off a LED.

89
00:07:14,920 --> 00:07:19,533
Save with Command S. In the bottom left Quickstart Panel, click Build. Check the

90
00:07:19,533 --> 00:07:21,320
bottom Console for zero errors.

91
00:07:21,820 --> 00:07:27,759
Plug the cable into the port marked SDA. The first time for this project, choose

92
00:07:27,759 --> 00:07:30,060
Run, then Debug Configurations.

93
00:07:30,560 --> 00:07:36,929
Right-click GDB PEMicro Interface Debugging, and choose New Configuration. Pick your

94
00:07:36,929 --> 00:07:40,720
P1 project, and the .axf file in its Debug folder.

95
00:07:41,220 --> 00:07:52,371
On the PEmicro Debugger tab, set Device to KL26Z128M4. Click Apply, then Debug, then

96
00:07:52,371 --> 00:07:53,300
Resume.

97
00:07:53,800 --> 00:08:01,288
Open your serial terminal at 115200, then press the board's Reset button. You should

98
00:08:01,288 --> 00:08:03,160
see the two messages.

99
00:08:04,160 --> 00:08:10,240
Press Terminate, the red square. Then switch on lines 244 to 253.

100
00:08:10,740 --> 00:08:21,084
Lines 244 to 246 fill in a settings form called sw_config. Digital input means this

101
00:08:21,084 --> 00:08:22,580
pin listens.

102
00:08:23,080 --> 00:08:26,600
The zero is a starting level, which an input ignores.

103
00:08:27,100 --> 00:08:35,821
Lines 248 to 250 fill in LED config. Digital output means this pin acts. Its

104
00:08:35,821 --> 00:08:38,460
starting level is zero.

105
00:08:38,960 --> 00:08:45,699
Line 252 hands the button form to the hardware. The and sign means: here is where

106
00:08:45,699 --> 00:08:47,280
the form is stored.

107
00:08:47,780 --> 00:08:51,300
Line 253 does the same for the light.

108
00:08:51,800 --> 00:08:54,760
Build and load. The light is zero. On or off?

109
00:08:55,260 --> 00:09:00,260
Your turn. Pause the video.

110
00:09:00,260 --> 00:09:03,140
On, because this light turns on when its pin is low.

111
00:09:04,140 --> 00:09:07,820
Switch on lines 289 and 290.

112
00:09:08,320 --> 00:09:11,120
Line 289 sets when to ring.

113
00:09:11,620 --> 00:09:17,229
It names the port, the pin, and falling edge: the moment the pin drops from high to

114
00:09:17,229 --> 00:09:18,580
low, when you press.

115
00:09:19,080 --> 00:09:25,560
Line 290, Enable IRQ, lets that ring reach the processor. You need both lines.

116
00:09:26,060 --> 00:09:32,043
Build, load and press. The handler now raises the flag, but nobody reads it yet, so

117
00:09:32,043 --> 00:09:33,340
you see no change.

118
00:09:34,340 --> 00:09:37,140
Switch on lines 319 to 329.

119
00:09:37,640 --> 00:09:43,400
Line 319, while one: repeat forever. Line 320 opens the loop.

120
00:09:43,900 --> 00:09:53,420
Line 321: if the flag is true, do what's in the next brackets. Line 322 opens them.

121
00:09:53,920 --> 00:09:57,760
Line 323 prints the button's name and says it was pressed.

122
00:09:58,260 --> 00:10:04,500
Line 325 toggles the light: on becomes off, off becomes on.

123
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:10,360
Line 327 sets the flag back to false, ready for the next press.

124
00:10:10,860 --> 00:10:16,460
Line 328 closes the if. Line 329 closes the loop.

125
00:10:16,960 --> 00:10:20,480
What happens if you delete line 327?

126
00:10:20,980 --> 00:10:25,980
Your turn. Pause the video.

127
00:10:25,980 --> 00:10:29,820
The flag stays true, so the loop keeps printing and toggling.

128
00:10:30,820 --> 00:10:37,770
Build, load, Resume, and press the button. The light switches, and the terminal says

129
00:10:37,770 --> 00:10:40,500
SW1 is pressed. Try five presses.

130
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:46,760
If one press sometimes acts like two, that's contact bounce. Demo seven fixes it.

131
00:10:47,760 --> 00:10:52,902
Follow one press all the way. Your finger pulls pin C 3 low. The port sees a falling

132
00:10:52,902 --> 00:10:55,840
edge. The processor pauses and runs the handler.

133
00:10:56,340 --> 00:11:06,075
Line 154 clears the signal, and line 156 raises the flag. The loop sees the flag at

134
00:11:06,075 --> 00:11:11,940
line 321. It prints, toggles, and lowers the flag.

135
00:11:12,940 --> 00:11:19,020
On line 289, change Falling to Rising. When will the light switch now?

136
00:11:19,520 --> 00:11:24,520
Your turn. Pause the video.

137
00:11:24,520 --> 00:11:25,880
When you let go.

138
00:11:26,380 --> 00:11:32,112
Try Either Edge. Press and release slowly and count the switches. Then restore

139
00:11:32,112 --> 00:11:32,700
Falling.

140
00:11:33,200 --> 00:11:39,429
Put two slashes in front of line 154, load, and press once. The handler runs again

141
00:11:39,429 --> 00:11:42,240
and again, and the loop gets no time.

142
00:11:42,740 --> 00:11:45,300
Remove the slashes and load again.

143
00:11:46,300 --> 00:11:53,061
Now explain it to a friend in one breath: a press rings a bell, a tiny function

144
00:11:53,061 --> 00:11:57,340
leaves a note, and the main loop acts on the note.

145
00:11:57,840 --> 00:12:03,520
If you can say that and point to the lines, you truly understand it. Well done.

